(60, 'Course Schedule', 'course-schedule', 'Medium', 'Graphs', '["Graphs", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '40.6%', 2360, 50, '<p>There are a total of numCourses courses you have to take, labeled from 0 to numCourses - 1. Return true if you can finish all courses.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> numCourses=2, prerequisites=[[1,0]]\n<strong>Output:</strong> true</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "numCourses=2, prerequisites=[[1,0]]"}, "expected": "true"}, {"input_params": {"input": "numCourses=2, prerequisites=[[1,0],[0,1]]"}, "expected": "false"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Course Schedule utilizes Graphs techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(61, 'Course Schedule II', 'course-schedule-ii', 'Medium', 'Graphs', '["Graphs", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '47.7%', 2391, 53, '<p>Return the ordering of courses you should take to finish all courses. If it is impossible to finish all courses, return an empty array.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> numCourses=2, prerequisites=[[1,0]]\n<strong>Output:</strong> [0,1]</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "numCourses=2, prerequisites=[[1,0]]"}, "expected": "[0,1]"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Course Schedule II utilizes Graphs techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(62, 'Word Ladder', 'word-ladder', 'Hard', 'Graphs', '["Graphs", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '54.8%', 2422, 56, '<p>Given two words, beginWord and endWord, and a dictionary wordList, return the number of words in the shortest transformation sequence.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> beginWord=\'hit\', endWord=\'cog\', wordList=[\'hot\',\'dot\',\'dog\',\'lot\',\'log\',\'cog\']\n<strong>Output:</strong> 5</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "beginWord=\'hit\', endWord=\'cog\', wordList=[\'hot\',\'dot\',\'dog\',\'lot\',\'log\',\'cog\']"}, "expected": "5"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Word Ladder utilizes Graphs techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(63, 'Climbing Stairs', 'climbing-stairs', 'Easy', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '61.0%', 2453, 59, '<p>You are climbing a staircase. It takes n steps to reach the top. Each time you can either climb 1 or 2 steps. In how many distinct ways can you climb to the top?</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> n=2\n<strong>Output:</strong> 2</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "n=2"}, "expected": "2"}, {"input_params": {"input": "n=3"}, "expected": "3"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Climbing Stairs utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(64, 'Min Cost Climbing Stairs', 'min-cost-climbing-stairs', 'Easy', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '68.1%', 2484, 62, '<p>You are given an integer array cost where cost[i] is the cost of ith step on a staircase. Return the minimum cost to reach the top of the floor.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> cost=[10,15,20]\n<strong>Output:</strong> 15</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "cost=[10,15,20]"}, "expected": "15"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Min Cost Climbing Stairs utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(65, 'House Robber', 'house-robber', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '40.2%', 2515, 65, '<p>Determine the maximum amount of money you can rob tonight without alerting the police (cannot rob adjacent houses).</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> nums=[1,2,3,1]\n<strong>Output:</strong> 4</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "nums=[1,2,3,1]"}, "expected": "4"}, {"input_params": {"input": "nums=[2,7,9,3,1]"}, "expected": "12"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for House Robber utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(66, 'House Robber II', 'house-robber-ii', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '47.3%', 2546, 68, '<p>All houses at this place are arranged in a circle. Return the maximum amount of money you can rob without alerting the police.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> nums=[2,3,2]\n<strong>Output:</strong> 3</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "nums=[2,3,2]"}, "expected": "3"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for House Robber II utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(67, 'Longest Palindromic Substring', 'longest-palindromic-substring', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '54.4%', 2577, 71, '<p>Given a string s, return the longest palindromic substring in s.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> s=\'babad\'\n<strong>Output:</strong> \'bab\'</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "s=\'babad\'"}, "expected": "\'bab\'"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Longest Palindromic Substring utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(68, 'Palindromic Substrings', 'palindromic-substrings', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '61.5%', 2608, 74, '<p>Given a string s, return the number of palindromic substrings in it.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> s=\'abc\'\n<strong>Output:</strong> 3</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "s=\'abc\'"}, "expected": "3"}, {"input_params": {"input": "s=\'aaa\'"}, "expected": "6"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Palindromic Substrings utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(69, 'Decode Ways', 'decode-ways', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '68.6%', 2639, 77, '<p>A message containing letters from A-Z can be encoded into numbers using the mapping A->1 ... Z->26. Return the number of ways to decode it.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> s=\'12\'\n<strong>Output:</strong> 2</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "s=\'12\'"}, "expected": "2"}, {"input_params": {"input": "s=\'226\'"}, "expected": "3"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Decode Ways utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(70, 'Coin Change', 'coin-change', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '40.7%', 2670, 80, '<p>Return the fewest number of coins that you need to make up that amount. If that amount of money cannot be made up by any combination of the coins, return -1.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> coins=[1,2,5], amount=11\n<strong>Output:</strong> 3</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "coins=[1,2,5], amount=11"}, "expected": "3"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Coin Change utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(71, 'Maximum Product Subarray', 'maximum-product-subarray', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '47.8%', 2701, 83, '<p>Given an integer array nums, find a subarray that has the largest product, and return the product.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> nums=[2,3,-2,4]\n<strong>Output:</strong> 6</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "nums=[2,3,-2,4]"}, "expected": "6"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Maximum Product Subarray utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
(72, 'Word Break', 'word-break', 'Medium', '1-D DP', '["1-D", "Algorithm", "Coding"]', '54.0%', 2732, 86, '<p>Given a string s and a dictionary of strings wordDict, return true if s can be segmented into a space-separated sequence of one or more dictionary words.</p>\n<div class="problem-example-card">\n  <h4>Example 1:</h4>\n  <pre><code><strong>Input:</strong> s=\'leetcode\', wordDict=[\'leet\',\'code\']\n<strong>Output:</strong> true</code></pre>\n</div>', '["1 <= input.length <= 10^5", "Values are within standard 32-bit integer limits.", "Optimized time complexity expected (O(N) or O(N log N))."]', '["Consider the edge cases such as empty input or single element.", "Think about using a suitable data structure for fast lookup."]', '{"python3": "class Solution:\\n    def solve(self, input_data):\\n        # Write your optimal solution here\\n        pass\\n", "javascript": "/**\\n * @param {any} inputData\\n * @return {any}\\n */\\nfunction solve(inputData) {\\n    // Write your optimal solution here\\n}\\n", "cpp": "class Solution {\\npublic:\\n    int solve(vector<int>& nums) {\\n        // Optimal C++ solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n};\\n", "java": "class Solution {\\n    public int solve(int[] nums) {\\n        // Optimal Java solution\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n}\\n"}', '[{"input_params": {"input": "s=\'leetcode\', wordDict=[\'leet\',\'code\']"}, "expected": "true"}]', '[{"input_params": {"input": "EdgeCase_1"}, "expected": "OptimalResult"}]', 'Standard approach for Word Break utilizes 1-D DP techniques with optimal time and space complexity.', '2026-11-07 05:50:32'),
